# The reference twin computes the same transformer bodies as the native
# adapter, over kernel.md section 4's footprint: what a transformer writes and
# what it reads. Both orderings below are one group with two non-central
# members, and the second is the case a message-only extent misses -- its
# message blocks are disjoint while its bodies interleave.
#
# Neither refuses. Whether an interleaved group decomposes is the precondition
# of composing two claims in parallel, and no shipped rule does that; what an
# artifact-level claim owes is that every round was accounted for, which
# interleaving-pin.test and body-interleave.test pin.
#
# REQUIRES: uv
# RUN: %uv python %S/Inputs/reference_interleaving.py | FileCheck %s
# CHECK: interleaved bodies [('opa', 0, 4, False), ('opb', 1, 5, False)]
# CHECK: interleaved group ['opa', 'opb'] non-central ['opa', 'opb']
# CHECK: separated bodies [('opa', 0, 5, False), ('opb', 2, 4, False)]
# CHECK: separated group ['opa', 'opb'] non-central ['opa', 'opb']
#
# A transformer owning no message at all still has a body: two shipped
# contracts declare a message-free round, and under a message-only criterion
# such a reduction had an empty extent and dropped out of the question.
# CHECK: message-free bodies [('batch', 5, 5, False)]
